# Expo Router

This project uses Expo Router. Inspect the existing routing architecture before making navigation changes.

## Route Structure
- Locate the actual app directory: app/ or src/app/.
- Inspect root and nested _layout.tsx files.
- Preserve route groups, dynamic segments, modal routes, and deep links.
- Keep reusable UI components outside the route directory where appropriate.
- Do not move or rename route files without checking their navigation effects.

## Layout Ownership
- Use existing Expo Router layouts to configure shared Stack, Tabs, headers, and screen options.
- Preserve the project's current tab implementation: JavaScript Tabs, Native Tabs, or custom tabs.
- Do not assume that all Expo Router tab implementations have identical safe-area or keyboard behavior.
- Do not recreate a tab bar or header inside an individual screen when the layout already provides it.
- Do not introduce a separate manually configured NavigationContainer into an existing Expo Router application.

## Navigation
- Use Expo Router APIs compatible with the installed version.
- Follow the existing conventions for Link, router navigation, route parameters, and typed routes.
- Preserve authentication redirects and route access rules.
- Avoid navigation loops, duplicate route definitions, and unnecessary nested layouts.

## Verification
- Check affected routes and their parent layouts.
- Verify back navigation, tabs, modals, and deep links where relevant.
- Preserve existing navigation behavior while improving screen design.
- Never claim route-level testing unless the relevant flows were exercised.